Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.43,0:00:05.31,Default,,0,0,0,,The explorer finds two lanterns representing a pair of\Nqubits We can prepare an entangled
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.33,0:00:09.97,Default,,0,0,0,,state using a Hadamard and a controlled inversion gate\Nknown as controlled NOT In this
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.98,0:00:14.72,Default,,0,0,0,,example measuring both qubits in the zero and one basis\Ncan produce zero zero or one one
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.44,0:00:19.95,Default,,0,0,0,,The pair's state cannot be described as two independent\Nindividual states However
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.04,0:00:24.25,Default,,0,0,0,,observing matching results in a single basis does not by\Nitself prove entanglement
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.49,0:00:29.08,Default,,0,0,0,,classical objects can also be correlated That is why our\Nlaboratory goes beyond this
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.09,0:00:32.51,Default,,0,0,0,,drawing and compares measurements in different bases\Nwhile knowing the circuit that
Dialogue: 0,0:00:32.52,0:00:33.26,Default,,0,0,0,,prepared the pair